{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "IDOR: Cross-account read of client task documents via /api/jobs/{id}/task-file",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "IDOR: Cross-account read of client task documents via /api/jobs/{id}/task-file"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `/api/jobs/{id}/task-file` endpoint issues a signed download URL for any job's attached client document to any authenticated user, with no ownership or applicant-relationship check. Any wallet-holder can create an account, enumerate job IDs from the public bootstrap, and download clients' proprietary task documents."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The `/api/jobs/{id}/task-file` endpoint issues a signed download URL for any job's attached client document to any authenticated user, with no ownership or applicant-relationship check. Any wallet-holder can create an account, enumerate job IDs from the public bootstrap, and download clients' proprietary task documents.\n\nConfidentiality of client-supplied task documents is not enforced per-owner. Any authenticated user (or automated agent farm — the platform is explicitly designed for agent participants) can bulk-walk live job IDs and download every client's proprietary task brief, SOP, or internal document. Because agent registration is public and low-friction, this enables systematic scraping of all task-document content on the marketplace within the signed-URL TTL window. Since job metadata (title, brief, acceptanceCriteria, task file name, task path) is already public, the marginal confidentiality loss is restricted to the attached document body itself.\n\nServer-side, before redirecting: resolve the session's `activeProfileId` and only return the signed URL when (a) the session's profile is the job's `clientProfileId`, OR (b) holds a non-withdrawn application on this job, OR (c) is the assigned provider/evaluator. Return 403 otherwise. Do not rely on the fact that job metadata itself is public — the attachment object is a distinct confidentiality boundary. Also consider lowering the signed-URL TTL from ~1h and naming the bucket path without stable UUIDs.",
                "markdown": "The `/api/jobs/{id}/task-file` endpoint issues a signed download URL for any job's attached client document to any authenticated user, with no ownership or applicant-relationship check. Any wallet-holder can create an account, enumerate job IDs from the public bootstrap, and download clients' proprietary task documents.\n\nConfidentiality of client-supplied task documents is not enforced per-owner. Any authenticated user (or automated agent farm — the platform is explicitly designed for agent participants) can bulk-walk live job IDs and download every client's proprietary task brief, SOP, or internal document. Because agent registration is public and low-friction, this enables systematic scraping of all task-document content on the marketplace within the signed-URL TTL window. Since job metadata (title, brief, acceptanceCriteria, task file name, task path) is already public, the marginal confidentiality loss is restricted to the attached document body itself.\n\nServer-side, before redirecting: resolve the session's `activeProfileId` and only return the signed URL when (a) the session's profile is the job's `clientProfileId`, OR (b) holds a non-withdrawn application on this job, OR (c) is the assigned provider/evaluator. Return 403 otherwise. Do not rely on the fact that job metadata itself is public — the attachment object is a distinct confidentiality boundary. Also consider lowering the signed-URL TTL from ~1h and naming the bucket path without stable UUIDs."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-284",
              "name": "Supabase anon key grants full CRUD on sessions/users/tasks/projects + cross-project data exposure (RLS missing)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Supabase anon key grants full CRUD on sessions/users/tasks/projects + cross-project data exposure (RLS missing)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Supabase anon key shipped in the WorkNet static bundle grants unauthenticated read access to the shared production database (project `eldrxpulpluelgapyfjs`) across at least three applications, plus demonstrated anonymous INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on core tables. Plaintext session tokens are stored readable; sibling applications' live data (user registries, XP/GPS histories, billing/usage/invoice tables) is equally readable from the internet. Only a minority of tables (`api_keys`, the 8 `_pactum` tables) have RLS enforced — confirmation the gap is a per-table misconfiguration, not a platform limitation."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The Supabase anon key shipped in the WorkNet static bundle grants unauthenticated read access to the shared production database (project `eldrxpulpluelgapyfjs`) across at least three applications, plus demonstrated anonymous INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on core tables. Plaintext session tokens are stored readable; sibling applications' live data (user registries, XP/GPS histories, billing/usage/invoice tables) is equally readable from the internet. Only a minority of tables (`api_keys`, the 8 `_pactum` tables) have RLS enforced — confirmation the gap is a per-table misconfiguration, not a platform limitation.\n\nUnauthenticated attacker with the bundle-shipped anon key can enumerate all users of the learning app (Discord usernames) and WorkNet profiles, read every session token in plaintext (currently all expired; any future login immediately writes a fresh plaintext token into the exposed table), modify role markers (role_dc), forge and delete sessions, read WorkNet runtime state (profiles_arcworker, jobs_arcworker, agents_arcworker, skills_arcworker) including owner links and agent wallet addresses, and read sibling projects' GPS coordinates and XP history. Cross-product confidentiality and integrity impact; availability not tested (no DoS probes).\n\n1. Enable RLS on every table the anon key reaches (sessions, users, users_learn, tasks, projects, profiles_arcworker, jobs_arcworker, agents_arcworker, skills_arcworker, wallet_nonces_arcworker, rain_users, user_progress, markets_cherry, profiles_sd). Owner-scoped policies: SELECT/UPDATE only where the caller's user id matches the key column; deny anon INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. 2. Move session storage out of any PostgREST-exposed schema — server-only schema or hashed tokens with rotation on read. 3. Rotate and invalidate all currently stored session tokens; enforce server-side expiry cleanup (February-era rows still present). 4. Split the shared database into per-project schemas so one project's anon key cannot resolve sibling tables. 5. Separately review WorkNet /api/bootstrap returning a random recent profile with no Cookie header.",
                "markdown": "The Supabase anon key shipped in the WorkNet static bundle grants unauthenticated read access to the shared production database (project `eldrxpulpluelgapyfjs`) across at least three applications, plus demonstrated anonymous INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on core tables. Plaintext session tokens are stored readable; sibling applications' live data (user registries, XP/GPS histories, billing/usage/invoice tables) is equally readable from the internet. Only a minority of tables (`api_keys`, the 8 `_pactum` tables) have RLS enforced — confirmation the gap is a per-table misconfiguration, not a platform limitation.\n\nUnauthenticated attacker with the bundle-shipped anon key can enumerate all users of the learning app (Discord usernames) and WorkNet profiles, read every session token in plaintext (currently all expired; any future login immediately writes a fresh plaintext token into the exposed table), modify role markers (role_dc), forge and delete sessions, read WorkNet runtime state (profiles_arcworker, jobs_arcworker, agents_arcworker, skills_arcworker) including owner links and agent wallet addresses, and read sibling projects' GPS coordinates and XP history. Cross-product confidentiality and integrity impact; availability not tested (no DoS probes).\n\n1. Enable RLS on every table the anon key reaches (sessions, users, users_learn, tasks, projects, profiles_arcworker, jobs_arcworker, agents_arcworker, skills_arcworker, wallet_nonces_arcworker, rain_users, user_progress, markets_cherry, profiles_sd). Owner-scoped policies: SELECT/UPDATE only where the caller's user id matches the key column; deny anon INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE. 2. Move session storage out of any PostgREST-exposed schema — server-only schema or hashed tokens with rotation on read. 3. Rotate and invalidate all currently stored session tokens; enforce server-side expiry cleanup (February-era rows still present). 4. Split the shared database into per-project schemas so one project's anon key cannot resolve sibling tables. 5. Separately review WorkNet /api/bootstrap returning a random recent profile with no Cookie header."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "10.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-284",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/284.html"
            },
